ugh a bunch of  procedural hoops  and pay a ton of money to boot in  addition  assuming various allegations against her  are true it appears that lohan is  or has been a hardcore kleptomaniac the  troubled star has been accused of  stealing from parties  a wide variety of friends a jewelry  store or two  the wardrobe departments of her own  movies and from ex-boyfriend igor  tarabasov  while it's not quite fair to pass  judgment when you don't know the full  story  it's probably safe to say that this  unfortunate reputation has made execs  and producers think twice about casting  lohan on their  projects of course a celebrity going off  the radar usually means more than just  appearing in fewer movies and tv shows  with that in mind it's worth asking why  has the media seemingly lost interest in  lindsay lohan  well it's partly because she lives in  dubai now which makes her a lot less  accessible to the american press  it also happens to be the case that  during the mid-2000s  celeb centric media developed a fixation  on famous young women who were  enthusiastic participants  in recreational nightlife activities  this included lohan herself  hotel chain heiress paris hilton future  tv personality nicole richie  and perhaps most notably kim kardashian  who swung her brief phase of notoriety  into a media and fashion empire that  carries on to this day  while the kardashian brand may have  flourished however  the media obsession with hilton richie  and lohan had pretty much run its course  by 2010.  that's probably because the public  quickly became worn out from hearing  about the same celebrities getting in  trouble  all the time but maybe after lohan  narrowly avoided felony charges and  hilton wound up doing jail time in 2007  the public stopped finding their  exploits quite so fun to follow  and so the media decided to focus its  attention elsewhere  intended as lindsay lohan's  groundbreaking transition  into edgy adult roles 2007's i know who  killed me was shredded by critics and  largely ignored by moviegoers worse  still the production had to contort  itself around  lohan's court-mandated rehab stints plus  a handful of other lohan related  obstacles although director chris  sivertson has since praised her acting  saying she's got an incredible amount of  raw talent and what she does on camera  seems  almost effortless within the first 10  minutes it becomes immediately obvious  that sivertsen very much wants i know  who killed me to be twin peaks firewalk  with me but is it really fair to label i  know who killed me as discount david  lynch  and toss it aside could it be that the  world of 2007 was simply too sick of  lindsay lohan to give the film an  honest chance well as critic charles  bromesko writes  the baggage lohan dropped off at set  only serve to deepen  and enrich the subtext of a stealth noir  gem one that gestures to a long by gone  era of movie stardom through the  framework of a cheaper and dirtier sort  of serial killer thriller  maybe this one does deserve another  chance after all  the micro budget brainchild of director  paul schrader and writer brett easton  alice the canyons was met with critical  scorn  and it wasn't exactly widely seen either  this isn't necessarily surprising  as it's an erotic thriller with a  serious mean streak  so not exactly mainstream fair still  the film's dead on arrival status proved  that controversy alone cannot always  carry a film to profit  the fact that lohan allegedly couldn't  be bothered to promote the film  probably didn't help matters but more  importantly  a few years after the movie's release a  deluge of sexual  assault allegations ruined the public's  ability to comfortably watch lohan's  canyon's co-star james dean pretend to  abuse a woman on screen  as such it doesn't seem like the canyons  stands much  chance of becoming an ironic cult  favorite anytime  soon but even before all that happened  the production had been caught up in the  ongoing torna